1
00:00:00,287 --> 00:00:01,147
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, ask yourself.

2
00:00:02,207 --> 00:00:03,368
[SPEAKER_00]: Can you stop your feeling?

3
00:00:03,428 --> 00:00:05,388
[SPEAKER_00]: Can you stop your form from deteriorating?

4
00:00:05,788 --> 00:00:07,989
[SPEAKER_00]: Can you prevent perceptions from besetting you?

5
00:00:09,309 --> 00:00:10,169
[SPEAKER_00]: Can you not choose?

6
00:00:17,771 --> 00:00:20,752
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, saṃsāra is usually translated as rebirth.

7
00:00:22,393 --> 00:00:23,973
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's really...

8
00:00:25,157 --> 00:00:32,419
[SPEAKER_00]: Literally, the meaning of saṃsāra means to wander on, keep wandering onwards, just keep going, keep going.

9
00:00:34,300 --> 00:00:37,721
[SPEAKER_00]: So... and it is better.

10
00:00:37,921 --> 00:00:42,403
[SPEAKER_00]: It certainly sheds clearer light on the idea of saṃsāra than the word rebirth.

11
00:00:43,463 --> 00:00:51,026
[SPEAKER_00]: Because rebirth means like this life, and then the next life, and then in that in between, it's just me sort of passing from one life to the other.

12
00:00:52,706 --> 00:00:54,607
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's not really that you're being reborn,

13
00:00:55,657 --> 00:01:01,601
[SPEAKER_00]: It's more like just you're still up, just here and there, and elsewhere, on and on and on.

14
00:01:02,041 --> 00:01:13,649
[SPEAKER_00]: The simile the Buddha would give is just like a man who went to one village, and from that village, later on, he went to another one, and then to another one, and then to another one, and then to another one.

15
00:01:13,909 --> 00:01:17,212
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's still like the man just going through all these different places.

16
00:01:17,752 --> 00:01:20,094
[SPEAKER_00]: You're here now, and then somewhere else.

17
00:01:20,754 --> 00:01:23,276
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, without these memories, without these recognitions,

18
00:01:24,412 --> 00:01:30,877
[SPEAKER_00]: But, same sort of set of aggregates, same point of view is just wandering on.

19
00:01:30,957 --> 00:01:34,760
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, you go to sleep, you wake up another day, then you go to sleep, you wake up as an animal.

20
00:01:35,840 --> 00:01:38,202
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, I'm overly simplifying it, but I want to illustrate it.

21
00:01:38,242 --> 00:01:44,767
[SPEAKER_00]: It's... that's like sometimes reflecting upon saṃsāra in the suttas would have an impact on people in a sense of, I better practice.

22
00:01:46,343 --> 00:01:49,265
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if you talk about rebirths, it's sort of out of your hands.

23
00:01:49,445 --> 00:01:51,287
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just how the universe works.

24
00:01:51,787 --> 00:01:54,609
[SPEAKER_00]: You just come, you're just reborn here and there.

25
00:01:55,130 --> 00:01:57,131
[SPEAKER_00]: But the birth in itself, it's not the beginning.

26
00:01:57,432 --> 00:02:07,640
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you think from that point of view, you realize, like, well, imagine, like, for example, if now, if somebody were to offer to you, would you start this life again?

27
00:02:08,600 --> 00:02:10,362
[SPEAKER_00]: Say, you go back and become a toddler?

28
00:02:11,429 --> 00:02:18,475
[SPEAKER_00]: Without having... being able to keep all the memories and everything you've been through, that you know now, that has to be erased.

29
00:02:18,495 --> 00:02:20,296
[SPEAKER_00]: Would you go through the same ordeal again?

30
00:02:20,696 --> 00:02:25,940
[SPEAKER_00]: Just in the life that you already know, let alone a realm that you are not familiar with, or something like that.

31
00:02:27,542 --> 00:02:39,111
[SPEAKER_00]: And actually, when you think about it, usually the appeal is behind, oh, I'll keep my memories and then I can start the same, like when people think about... And that's when they think about the rebirth, as if, you know, you take things with you and so on, and then...

32
00:02:40,033 --> 00:02:41,514
[SPEAKER_00]: You better yourself through rebirth.

33
00:02:41,554 --> 00:02:41,694
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

34
00:02:41,855 --> 00:02:45,798
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just erasing, but you're still there.

35
00:02:45,878 --> 00:02:48,159
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's just somebody wipes you clean and then you start again.

36
00:02:50,021 --> 00:02:51,142
[SPEAKER_00]: And knowledge is gone as well.

37
00:02:51,602 --> 00:02:54,584
[SPEAKER_00]: The stuff that you learn and experienced.

38
00:02:55,385 --> 00:02:56,666
[SPEAKER_00]: So, would you want to do it again then?

39
00:02:57,727 --> 00:02:59,848
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, it will make you think twice.

40
00:03:00,789 --> 00:03:03,391
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you might make even worse choices than you did in your life.

41
00:03:03,411 --> 00:03:08,535
[SPEAKER_00]: That pressure of having to choose, having to act, being in the environment, it's...

42
00:03:10,312 --> 00:03:13,533
[SPEAKER_00]: In hindsight, it can be pleasant, but when it's there, it's never pleasant.

43
00:03:14,093 --> 00:03:36,841
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you realize that saṃsāra is that point of view that you are bound with, and that you will, like, unless you develop wisdom and practice dhamma correctly, that you will be then just repeating it on and on, just wiping the slate clean here and there and elsewhere, but still going through the same things on and on and on and on.

44
00:03:36,861 --> 00:03:37,642
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's what Buddha meant.

45
00:03:37,662 --> 00:03:38,342
[SPEAKER_00]: There is nobody

46
00:03:39,730 --> 00:03:41,791
[SPEAKER_00]: There is nothing that you haven't been already.

47
00:03:41,871 --> 00:03:45,113
[SPEAKER_00]: Beings have been in saṃsāra for so long that you've been everything.

48
00:03:45,153 --> 00:03:49,976
[SPEAKER_00]: You've been mother, and the father, and the killer, and the killed, and every animal.

49
00:03:49,996 --> 00:03:51,216
[SPEAKER_00]: You've been everything.

50
00:03:51,276 --> 00:03:52,337
[SPEAKER_00]: You've been around so long.

51
00:03:52,357 --> 00:03:58,560
[SPEAKER_00]: The tedious repetition that you have no memory of, that's basically what saṃsāra is.

52
00:03:59,701 --> 00:04:04,323
[SPEAKER_00]: So then you realize this life is not like, oh, now I'm born, saṃsāra is waiting for me.

53
00:04:04,403 --> 00:04:04,524
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

54
00:04:04,624 --> 00:04:07,105
[SPEAKER_00]: This is already that wandering on.

55
00:04:07,205 --> 00:04:08,906
[SPEAKER_00]: It's one of many wandering on.

56
00:04:10,063 --> 00:04:15,985
[SPEAKER_00]: And the directions you choose, based on your actions and choices you make, are the directions you will wander on later.

57
00:04:16,665 --> 00:04:17,685
[SPEAKER_00]: It's setting you up for it.

58
00:04:17,905 --> 00:04:25,308
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why beings are the owners of directions and, you know, the actions you... the choices you make, things you do, determine then where you're heading.

59
00:04:32,110 --> 00:04:33,930
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, you are not in saṃsāra.

60
00:04:34,110 --> 00:04:36,051
[SPEAKER_00]: Saṃsāra is not the universe that you inhabit.

61
00:04:36,851 --> 00:04:38,772
[SPEAKER_00]: Saṃsāra is your experience as a whole.

62
00:04:39,754 --> 00:04:42,735
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what you have now, is what saṃsāra is.

63
00:04:44,636 --> 00:04:50,279
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in the same sense, you can recognize certain tedious repetition of, like, day-to-day things.

64
00:04:50,739 --> 00:04:54,140
[SPEAKER_00]: Just extend that into infinite.

65
00:04:55,381 --> 00:04:58,922
[SPEAKER_00]: Extend it, like, into the next life, and next life, and so on.

66
00:04:59,623 --> 00:05:02,604
[SPEAKER_00]: Day, month, year, hundred years, is just repetition.

67
00:05:03,544 --> 00:05:09,367
[SPEAKER_00]: Perpetually just repeating, because you kind of... you forget, you lapse, you lose the memory of.

68
00:05:11,229 --> 00:05:11,989
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's what Buddha said.

69
00:05:12,029 --> 00:05:22,072
[SPEAKER_00]: Even if somebody can remember the previous lives, the previous wanderings on, the directions they've been in, all they would remember is these five aggregates that they have now.

70
00:05:22,412 --> 00:05:29,673
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the five aggregates, which is your experience as a whole, matter, feeling, perception, intentions, consciousness, there is nothing outside of that.

71
00:05:29,973 --> 00:05:30,814
[SPEAKER_00]: Saṃsāra is that.

72
00:05:31,194 --> 00:05:37,975
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the aggregates that are bound with ignorance to keep repeating basically infinitely.

73
00:05:38,135 --> 00:05:40,936
[SPEAKER_00]: Until you make the effort and maybe uproot that vision.

74
00:05:47,713 --> 00:05:50,875
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, the content changes while the aggregates remain the same.

75
00:05:53,057 --> 00:05:53,878
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's the whole point.

76
00:05:55,419 --> 00:05:57,460
[SPEAKER_00]: This village, that village...

77
00:05:57,480 --> 00:06:00,182
[SPEAKER_00]: The point is, you are still wandering on.

78
00:06:00,843 --> 00:06:05,706
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's what I mean, you don't have... And it's not like, oh, this is, metaphorically speaking, samsāra is already here.

79
00:06:06,227 --> 00:06:06,347
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

80
00:06:06,447 --> 00:06:11,571
[SPEAKER_00]: Factually, this, what this life already is, it's already wandering on.

81
00:06:12,191 --> 00:06:13,072
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, ask yourself.

82
00:06:14,122 --> 00:06:15,263
[SPEAKER_00]: Can you stop your feeling?

83
00:06:15,323 --> 00:06:17,286
[SPEAKER_00]: Can you stop your form from deteriorating?

84
00:06:17,686 --> 00:06:19,889
[SPEAKER_00]: Can you prevent perceptions from besetting you?

85
00:06:21,210 --> 00:06:22,071
[SPEAKER_00]: Can you not choose?

86
00:06:22,231 --> 00:06:23,032
[SPEAKER_00]: Can you not become?

87
00:06:23,052 --> 00:06:24,374
[SPEAKER_00]: Can you just switch off your consciousness?

88
00:06:24,394 --> 00:06:25,895
[SPEAKER_00]: You have no say in any of these aggregates.

89
00:06:25,916 --> 00:06:27,057
[SPEAKER_00]: They're just flowing already.

90
00:06:28,118 --> 00:06:29,399
[SPEAKER_00]: Coming and going, so to speak.

91
00:06:29,480 --> 00:06:30,361
[SPEAKER_00]: Taking directions.

92
00:06:32,702 --> 00:06:33,983
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's already what saṃsāra is.

93
00:06:34,623 --> 00:06:49,192
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't need the belief and proof of next life, because you're already not in control of these aggregates, and they're already taking you in directions that often you don't even want to go, and certainly forcing you to endure things.

94
00:06:50,100 --> 00:06:51,361
[SPEAKER_00]: So, here's the saṃsāra.

95
00:06:52,162 --> 00:06:53,703
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't get it more directly than that.

96
00:06:54,344 --> 00:06:55,645
[SPEAKER_00]: So, yeah, death is not the end.

97
00:06:56,225 --> 00:06:57,867
[SPEAKER_00]: And birth is not the beginning.

98
00:06:58,107 --> 00:07:00,749
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just like rearrangement of the same thing.

99
00:07:01,189 --> 00:07:02,691
[SPEAKER_00]: Another way of repeating it.

100
00:07:03,091 --> 00:07:13,560
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you don't need a memory of previous lives or like a special insight into future lives to see saṃsāra as wandering on already something you're fully engaged as we speak.

101
00:07:20,171 --> 00:07:30,636
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, yeah, you are doing it and you can't just stop it, because there will be another choice within the saṃsāra, another choice within the aggregate of choice, within the aggregate of saṅkhara.

102
00:07:30,776 --> 00:07:31,517
[SPEAKER_00]: Can you not choose?

103
00:07:31,877 --> 00:07:34,658
[SPEAKER_00]: Can you abstain from choice without that being another choice?

104
00:07:36,239 --> 00:07:36,579
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't.

105
00:07:36,739 --> 00:07:37,600
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you're fully within it.

106
00:07:38,040 --> 00:07:38,720
[SPEAKER_00]: You have to do it.

107
00:07:40,821 --> 00:07:41,562
[SPEAKER_00]: Can you not feel?

108
00:07:42,773 --> 00:07:43,813
[SPEAKER_00]: Is that even conceivable?

109
00:07:44,193 --> 00:07:50,375
[SPEAKER_00]: Can you imagine your existence, in the most abstract terms, without the form, without the body enduring there?

110
00:07:51,015 --> 00:07:51,515
[SPEAKER_00]: Impossible.

111
00:07:51,615 --> 00:07:52,215
[SPEAKER_00]: Inconceivable.

112
00:07:54,035 --> 00:08:00,737
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you're fully under the weight and control of the aggregates, for which direction you're only responsible.

113
00:08:01,437 --> 00:08:05,638
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, you can't control them, you can't stop them, you can't tell your aggregates go this way or that way.

114
00:08:06,861 --> 00:08:13,206
[SPEAKER_00]: But you can only then start making the choices of a wholesome kind, as opposed to choices of unwholesome kind.

115
00:08:13,666 --> 00:08:17,930
[SPEAKER_00]: And in that indirect manner, maybe improve the state of your aggregates, and so on.

116
00:08:17,990 --> 00:08:22,093
[SPEAKER_00]: And you can again, not in six lifetimes, in this life as well.

117
00:08:23,094 --> 00:08:28,358
[SPEAKER_00]: If a person starts practicing sensory strength, they will naturally, their mind will develop more patience.

118
00:08:30,070 --> 00:08:33,871
[SPEAKER_00]: More calmness, more self-composure, more self-restraint and strength.

119
00:08:34,531 --> 00:08:41,033
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's already something that will make this wandering on in this life much more endurable, much more agreeable as well.

120
00:08:41,573 --> 00:08:43,914
[SPEAKER_00]: Would... like, is being impatient suffering?

121
00:08:44,834 --> 00:08:46,514
[SPEAKER_00]: Is being impatient pleasant or unpleasant?

122
00:08:47,034 --> 00:08:47,775
[SPEAKER_00]: It's very unpleasant.

123
00:08:48,655 --> 00:08:49,855
[SPEAKER_00]: When you want something, you can't get it.

124
00:08:50,795 --> 00:08:52,956
[SPEAKER_00]: Or you don't want something, but you can't get it, you have to endure it.

125
00:08:53,416 --> 00:08:54,156
[SPEAKER_00]: You're not patient.

126
00:08:55,237 --> 00:08:56,397
[SPEAKER_00]: But is that patience given?

127
00:08:57,199 --> 00:08:58,300
[SPEAKER_00]: There's just one example.

128
00:08:58,340 --> 00:09:02,224
[SPEAKER_00]: So, by making choices of wholesome kind in this life, by not pursuing sensuality, sort of,

129
00:09:16,892 --> 00:09:29,200
[SPEAKER_00]: Carelessly, without any restraint, without any regard for others, not being immoral, not being non-virtuous, not being harmful to others, by making that effort to keep yourself in check, you're actually developing patience.

130
00:09:29,240 --> 00:09:36,305
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means, the endurance of this saṃsāra, of this wandering on, you will be able to tolerate it more.

131
00:09:36,345 --> 00:09:37,206
[SPEAKER_00]: The pressure as well.

132
00:09:38,406 --> 00:09:39,227
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you learned it.

133
00:09:39,327 --> 00:09:40,548
[SPEAKER_00]: You developed that tolerance.

134
00:09:41,742 --> 00:09:42,302
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's the thing.

135
00:09:42,342 --> 00:09:44,142
[SPEAKER_00]: Tolerance can... needs to be developed.

136
00:09:44,183 --> 00:09:46,823
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not God-given quality.

137
00:09:48,023 --> 00:09:49,704
[SPEAKER_00]: If you invest effort in it, it will grow.

138
00:09:49,824 --> 00:09:50,804
[SPEAKER_00]: If you don't, it will shrink.

139
00:09:51,264 --> 00:09:57,285
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, you will be even more pulled by the aggregates, by the desires, by the feelings.

140
00:09:58,025 --> 00:10:03,326
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, your wandering on will be even faster, more hectic, more distracted, like an animal.

141
00:10:05,567 --> 00:10:10,848
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why, when rearrangement of the... these... this form occurs,

142
00:10:11,807 --> 00:10:14,016
[SPEAKER_00]: But your mind was the mind of an animal, pretty much.

143
00:10:14,879 --> 00:10:16,465
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the form you're going to be assuming.

144
00:10:18,070 --> 00:10:18,830
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's what I said.

145
00:10:18,850 --> 00:10:21,712
[SPEAKER_00]: If somebody behaves like an animal in this life, that's the realm they're heading.

146
00:10:22,412 --> 00:10:24,133
[SPEAKER_00]: If you're behaving even worse, that's the realm.

147
00:10:24,493 --> 00:10:24,633
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

148
00:10:24,673 --> 00:10:27,535
[SPEAKER_00]: Not the realm, like, again, figure of speech.

149
00:10:27,615 --> 00:10:29,596
[SPEAKER_00]: Not the realm as this place that waits for you.

150
00:10:29,656 --> 00:10:41,382
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just, you're wandering on, when it has this, like, a wiping of that slate clean and restarting it, that will be the form, that will be the means of engagement that it assumes, because that's what the mind has been cultivating.

151
00:10:41,402 --> 00:10:42,703
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that's what the body is, by the way.

152
00:10:43,283 --> 00:10:46,346
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like means of engagement with the world and the sense objects.

153
00:10:46,826 --> 00:10:54,233
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what you chose to engage with, the way you chose to engage with your body, that's basically where your mind is heading.

154
00:10:54,473 --> 00:11:02,561
[SPEAKER_00]: So, next time you might get something... you might get a body that's more suitable for the level of engagement that you want and expect.

155
00:11:04,487 --> 00:11:12,414
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if your mind was engaging with sense objects on the level of, like, animal passion and madness and thirst, you're gonna get a body that's much more suited.

156
00:11:12,434 --> 00:11:15,317
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why dogs have a smell 500 times stronger than humans.

157
00:11:16,078 --> 00:11:21,603
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that's how much... how many times they're more pulled by the sense objects than humans.

158
00:11:21,643 --> 00:11:22,244
[SPEAKER_00]: 500 times.

159
00:11:24,566 --> 00:11:27,128
[SPEAKER_00]: You might smell something nice, but will not make you lose your mind.

160
00:11:27,368 --> 00:11:28,790
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, dog will lose its mind.

161
00:11:30,508 --> 00:11:40,898
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you don't make the effort and you start giving in to losing your mind on account of sense objects, yeah, you're gonna get senses, then they will support that what you want, basically, what you keep choosing.

162
00:11:41,319 --> 00:11:48,085
[SPEAKER_00]: Or if you, like, develop sort of choices, they will abstain from sense objects, then you don't need sense organs.

163
00:11:49,246 --> 00:11:54,648
[SPEAKER_00]: So, when the rearrangement happens, so to speak, you're gonna get much less of sense organs.

164
00:11:55,449 --> 00:12:05,153
[SPEAKER_00]: So, like in the suttas, when the Buddha would describe the celestial type of bodies, that they're not... they're much more refined, not... you wouldn't even call them bodies from our point of view, what the body is.

165
00:12:05,413 --> 00:12:09,134
[SPEAKER_00]: But again, future life, previous life, doesn't matter.

166
00:12:09,534 --> 00:12:13,476
[SPEAKER_00]: What matters is to recognize the nature of aimless wandering on.

167
00:12:15,197 --> 00:12:18,178
[SPEAKER_00]: And any aim you might have within that wandering on,

168
00:12:18,710 --> 00:12:19,853
[SPEAKER_00]: It's fundamentally aimless.

169
00:12:20,173 --> 00:12:25,424
[SPEAKER_00]: So, even if you have some great purpose in this life, mundane purpose, not purpose of enlightenment,

170
00:12:26,684 --> 00:12:32,046
[SPEAKER_00]: It's... it's... it's a mundane purpose, which means it's not... it's not really changing the state of that aimless wandering on.

171
00:12:32,847 --> 00:12:42,031
[SPEAKER_00]: So, your aim is only relative and when the time comes for... for things to rearrange, they will and you will... that aim will make... make... make no difference.

172
00:12:42,451 --> 00:12:47,873
[SPEAKER_00]: In other words, you cannot prevent form from deforming, getting sick and dying.

173
00:12:47,893 --> 00:12:48,674
[SPEAKER_00]: It's inconceivable.

174
00:12:49,914 --> 00:12:51,575
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you don't borrow time every time.

175
00:12:52,665 --> 00:13:03,890
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you if you want to have like a like a simplified example of what would correspond to rebirth, it's not about being born again or dying, it's really about losing the memory.

176
00:13:04,170 --> 00:13:11,834
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why beings are bound to like constant pointless repetition of the same things, because they have no recollection that they've already done this.

177
00:13:13,078 --> 00:13:14,079
[SPEAKER_00]: Billions of times.

178
00:13:14,339 --> 00:13:16,600
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if you did, you wouldn't want to do it anymore.

179
00:13:16,700 --> 00:13:18,961
[SPEAKER_00]: You would really want to leave that state of affairs.

180
00:13:19,001 --> 00:13:21,222
[SPEAKER_00]: Imagine now somebody comes and wipes your memory.

181
00:13:21,462 --> 00:13:22,083
[SPEAKER_00]: Just the memory.

182
00:13:22,343 --> 00:13:23,563
[SPEAKER_00]: So your body is still intact.

183
00:13:24,144 --> 00:13:24,804
[SPEAKER_00]: You're still alive.

184
00:13:25,304 --> 00:13:30,607
[SPEAKER_00]: But completely, utterly, your memory, recognition, anything, everything you know, including yourself, is gone.

185
00:13:31,627 --> 00:13:38,191
[SPEAKER_00]: So for you, if you were to wake up from that state, although the... like somebody who can see externally would see, yeah, it's still the same person, same body.

186
00:13:38,231 --> 00:13:42,393
[SPEAKER_00]: But from your point of view, the body, everything, it would be a new thing.

187
00:13:43,436 --> 00:13:44,779
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's not a new thing, isn't it?

188
00:13:45,440 --> 00:13:49,828
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's a new thing from that internal point of view, because you have no memory of it.

189
00:13:50,129 --> 00:13:51,551
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's exactly what rebirth is.

190
00:13:52,785 --> 00:13:57,189
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just losing the memory of recognizing that it's not and the aggregates are not new, never were.

191
00:13:57,309 --> 00:13:58,890
[SPEAKER_00]: You're just gonna keep going and going.

192
00:13:59,270 --> 00:14:01,512
[SPEAKER_00]: Ignorance in regard to it has no beginning.

193
00:14:01,532 --> 00:14:05,675
[SPEAKER_00]: So that's why, like, you should be concerned about death and dying.

194
00:14:05,695 --> 00:14:13,662
[SPEAKER_00]: It's because everything you learn, even the wisdom you got, unless it was of a substantial kind, will most likely not play much part.

195
00:14:13,962 --> 00:14:18,486
[SPEAKER_00]: Then all you can do, and that's what the Buddha would say, like, if people wanted to have a better rebirth,

196
00:14:19,076 --> 00:14:20,797
[SPEAKER_00]: You focus on doing wholesome things.

197
00:14:21,658 --> 00:14:31,845
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, as I explained, simply by cultivating certain habits in the mind, when your memory is wiped clean, you're gonna start using the body from the point of view of the habits you cultivated before.

198
00:14:33,226 --> 00:14:37,109
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you don't have explicit memory of the habits, but the inclinations are there.

199
00:14:38,170 --> 00:14:47,537
[SPEAKER_00]: And inclinations, in this life, yeah, you're responsible for, but as inclinations, they're probably because you've been choosing those things many times before.

200
00:14:49,448 --> 00:14:53,012
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it doesn't mean that you can now just carelessly give in to inclinations.

201
00:14:53,653 --> 00:14:57,057
[SPEAKER_00]: It just means the presence of inclination is due to previous habits.

202
00:14:57,077 --> 00:14:58,899
[SPEAKER_00]: Which then also means you could reshape them.

203
00:14:58,919 --> 00:15:04,365
[SPEAKER_00]: If your inclinations are bad, double down in doing good things in this life, so you develop good inclinations.

204
00:15:05,600 --> 00:15:13,764
[SPEAKER_00]: So then, in the next life, those inclinations will be what inclines you and directs you, without that wisdom, without memory to recall on.

205
00:15:13,964 --> 00:15:20,487
[SPEAKER_00]: So that's really what that rebirth within saṃsāra is, and making you start again without knowing that you're starting again.

206
00:15:21,407 --> 00:15:22,488
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like the best trick.

